  39: ## T2 — Server attempt and sender fencing with key correlation
  40: Reasoning: Every outbox guarantee (exactly once, no late duplicate, no cross-user send) rests on a server 
contract that does not exist yet; it must be proven at integration level before any client queue relies on it.
  41: Task: The create route accepts an attempt generation and an expected sender for keyed creates; it commits only 
when the generation is current for (sender, conversation, key) at commit time and rejects a sender mismatch without 
storing. A new fence/lookup route, for the caller's own key only, atomically returns the stored message or makes every 
lower generation unable to commit, rejects older generations, never creates a message, and rejects anon and other 
users' keys. The original submitted composition is stored for replay comparison. Create and fence responses carry 
`clientMessageId`; outcome codes follow BR-005 (403 `NOT_PARTICIPANT` incl. missing conversation, 400, 413, 409, 
stale-generation). Unkeyed legacy creates and existing route/RPC signatures keep working.
  42: Done when: AC-044 (server part) and AC-045 cases (a)–(c) hold against local Postgres and the real routes, with 
at most one stored message per key in every case.
  43: Verify by: New messaging DB integration tests for AC-044 (crafted mismatched sender, anon fence, other-user 
key), AC-045 (row-lock-blocked insert, held stale request, stale fence), and AC-046 server status codes pass; legacy 
unkeyed create test passes; RLS review gate recorded.
  44: Covers: FR-006, FR-011, FR-034, BR-001, BR-004, BR-005, AC-044, AC-045, AC-046, AC-035
  45: Root: T2
  46: Depends on: —
